{"moveId":"pro-laws-remain-unexplained","type":"fallacy","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"argument-from-ignorance","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":"Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-laws-remain-unexplained","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"evolution and biological design","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that Darwinian explanation leaves a deeper question unanswered because the physical laws enabling evolution still require an account of their origin.","lockedProposition":"Collins argues that explaining evolution through the laws of physics leaves unanswered where those laws came from.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"but you said that well it's happened just because of the laws of physics i want to take this back to that step where did those come from well","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins concedes the bottom-up evolutionary account rather than resisting it, then cleanly distinguishes explaining biological complexity under physical laws from explaining why those enabling laws obtain. Principal limitation: A genuine residual question is not yet evidence for divine authorship, and the brief challenge offers neither a comparative causal argument nor a reason that the laws require an external personal source. Live burden: Collins must convert the foundational question into positive, discriminating warrant while meeting the objection that a creator may relocate rather than resolve the explanatory demand."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Collins asks where physical laws came from but does not answer that God authored them in this move. The assessment likewise says the residual question is not yet divine evidence, so ignorance is not made a premise for truth."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Collins asks where the enabling physical laws came from without asserting in this move that their unexplained origin proves divine authorship. The assessment identifies a residual question and warns against a later unsupported inference, not an ignorance inference already made."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-transcendent-mind-exceeds-concepts","type":"fallacy","label":"Special pleading","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"special-pleading","label":"Special pleading","definition":"Applying an exception or different standard to one side of the argument without giving a principled reason for the difference.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-transcendent-mind-exceeds-concepts","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"bottom-up simplicity and divine complexity","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that ordinary human complexity concepts inadequately model a transcendent divine mind whose capabilities would radically exceed our limited imaginative categories.","lockedProposition":"Collins argues that human attempts to imagine a transcendent divine mind would be radically inadequate relative to its capabilities.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"ability we humans have to try to imagine what god is really like if god exists and i believe he does it's got to be so completely pathetic compared to the reality of that complexity and that um awesome capability as a physicist mathematician","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins appropriately resists assuming that a proposed transcendent cause must resemble a biological brain or engineered machine, and he candidly emphasizes severe limits in human models of divine mentality. Principal limitation: Appealing to conceptual inadequacy does not explain why immense capability carries no explanatory cost, and it threatens asymmetry if positive claims about God remain admissible while critical complexity questions are declared inapplicable. Live burden: Collins must provide enough content to make the creator hypothesis intelligible and evidentially assessable while explaining which ordinary complexity inferences fail and why."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The assessment warns that conceptual inadequacy could become asymmetric insulation, but this move does not yet explicitly admit favorable claims while exempting God from an otherwise applicable standard without a reason."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The claim that ordinary models may inadequately describe a transcendent mind offers a principled difference in subject matter. The assessment warns that asymmetry would arise if positive claims remained admissible while criticism was excluded, but does not find that full asymmetry established here."}]}
{"moveId":"con-divine-simplicity-implausible","type":"fallacy","label":"Begging the question","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"begging-the-question","label":"Begging the question","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-divine-simplicity-implausible","side":"con","sectionTitle":"bottom-up simplicity and divine complexity","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Dawkins concludes that divine simplicity is implausible because a being capable of selecting fundamental constants must possess complexity corresponding to that sophisticated capacity.","lockedProposition":"Theological claims that God is simple are implausible because a being capable of choosing the fundamental constants would require corresponding complexity.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"god's got to be complex um i mean i i have come across the theologians who say the beautiful the beauty of god i think uh swinburne uh rich richard swinburne theologian says god is totally simple that's the beauty of it you don't need a complex god he is simple and that's ridiculous because if he is simple he couldn't invent the fundamental constants of the laws of physics and","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Dawkins does not merely repeat that God is complex; he offers a capability-based reason, arguing that choosing a life-permitting physical structure appears to require extensive informational or discriminatory resources. Principal limitation: The inference assumes that functional range entails internal composition and dismisses divine simplicity without engaging its claim that multiple divine acts arise from one noncomposite actuality rather than separable mechanisms. Live burden: Dawkins must show why selection among constants requires complexity under a defensible account applicable to a timeless agent, not only to engineered or biological systems."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Dawkins assumes functional sophistication entails internal complexity, but that disputed bridge is an unsupported premise rather than a restatement of the conclusion. The assessment identifies a missing complexity account, not circularity."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Dawkins's inference from sophisticated capability to complexity is insufficiently defended and may use the wrong complexity metric, but it is offered as a substantive premise rather than merely restating the conclusion. A disputed premise is not necessarily circular."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-multiverse-also-evidential-leap","type":"bias","label":"Ambiguity effect","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"ambiguity-effect","label":"Ambiguity effect","definition":"Preferring clearer or more familiar claims over uncertain ones, even when the uncertain option may deserve serious consideration.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/ambiguity-effect/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-multiverse-also-evidential-leap","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"lawful intelligibility, cosmic origins, and fine-tuning","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that the anthropic multiverse also requires an evidential leap because its many universes may remain experimentally inaccessible despite explaining observer selection.","lockedProposition":"An infinite multiverse supplies an anthropic alternative to design, but Collins argues that its expected experimental inaccessibility also requires an evidential leap.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"alternative of course is to postulate that there are a infinite number of other universes that have different settings of those particular constants and we could only be in one where it happened to work so that's why we're observing it from here of course we don't expect there will ever be an experimental means of actually detecting the presence of those universes so that's a a bit of a leap of faith as well but i go a little beyond","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins acknowledges how a multiverse would explain our observation through selection effects and appropriately compares theistic fine-tuning with a serious naturalistic competitor rather than claiming design follows automatically. Principal limitation: Experimental inaccessibility does not make rival hypotheses evidentially equivalent, since a multiverse may receive indirect support from independently successful physics, and the prediction of permanent undetectability is asserted rather than demonstrated. Live burden: Collins must compare theoretical motivation, simplicity, likelihoods, and priors instead of treating direct observability as the sole measure of rational warrant."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Collins emphasizes the uncertainty and expected inaccessibility of other universes, but he recognizes the multiverse as a serious anthropic alternative. The assessment criticizes his evidential comparison, not a demonstrated preference caused by ambiguity itself."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Collins emphasizes the multiverse's experimental inaccessibility, but he acknowledges its observer-selection explanation and calls both alternatives evidential leaps. The assessment faults an incomplete evidential comparison, not a demonstrated preference caused by ambiguity itself."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-timeless-god-stops-regress","type":"fallacy","label":"Special pleading","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"special-pleading","label":"Special pleading","definition":"Applying an exception or different standard to one side of the argument without giving a principled reason for the difference.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-timeless-god-stops-regress","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"lawful intelligibility, cosmic origins, and fine-tuning","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that a creator outside space and time needs no temporal beginning, thereby blocking the specific infinite regress applicable to temporally originated beings.","lockedProposition":"A creator outside space and time need not have a temporal beginning, so Collins argues that the same infinite regress does not arise.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"again i think of god as being outside space and time so all of our arguments about how you have to have a beginning and end don't apply anymore because if god is actually locked into space and time you've got an infinite regress problem but i don't think that's the case once you postulate a much broader sense of what the existence of god would be like","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins supplies a recognizable answer to the question “who created God” by distinguishing a temporal beginning regress from the proposed existence of an atemporal creator. Principal limitation: Atemporality addresses when God began but not why a capable mind exists, why it has particular intentions, or whether its explanatory complexity requires accounting, so it narrows Dawkins’s objection more than it defeats it. Live burden: Collins must defend the extra-temporal premise independently and explain why it terminates explanatory dependence without merely stipulating that the preferred hypothesis receives an exemption."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Although the assessment warns against stipulating an exemption, Collins offers atemporality as the relevant principled difference for a specifically temporal regress. That answer is incomplete but not an unexplained double standard."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"An atemporal being would not require a temporal beginning for the same reason temporally originated beings do, providing a relevant proposed distinction. The assessment says this premise needs independent defense but does not establish an exception lacking any principled basis."}]}
{"moveId":"con-constant-sensitivity","type":"fallacy","label":"Appeal to authority","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"appeal-to-authority","label":"Appeal to authority","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/appeal-to-authority/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-constant-sensitivity","side":"con","sectionTitle":"lawful intelligibility, cosmic origins, and fine-tuning","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Dawkins concludes that physical constants present a genuine explanatory datum because small alterations would prevent galaxies, stars, chemistry, and life’s necessary conditions.","lockedProposition":"Dawkins says small changes to the physical constants would prevent galaxies, stars, chemistry, and the prerequisites for life.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"physicists agree most physicists agree that if you if you change any of those constants by even a very very small amount then we we we don't come into existence the universe doesn't come into existence they have to be like that in order for galaxies to form for stars to form for chemistry to form actually um and and then for um for the prerequisite for life to evolve uh needs that as well so","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Dawkins develops the fine-tuning datum in a strong opposing form, tracing the dependence of life through galaxies, stars, and chemistry while resisting any attempt to minimize its importance. Principal limitation: The broad claim about changing any constant by a very small amount is undocumented and may obscure differences among parameters, viable ranges, compensating variations, and unresolved probability measures. Live burden: Dawkins must preserve this substantial concession while showing why sensitivity does not uniquely favor a tuner and what evidentially supported natural competitor can explain observer-compatible values."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Dawkins relies on the asserted agreement of most physicists to support sweeping sensitivity claims without presenting the relevant evidence or establishing the precise consensus; the assessment specifically calls the claim undocumented."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Dawkins mentions physicists' agreement but also supplies the evidential chain involving galaxies, stars, chemistry, and life. The assessment criticizes documentation and overbreadth, not reliance on expert status as though it alone settled fine-tuning."}]}
{"moveId":"con-fine-tuner-does-not-explain","type":"fallacy","label":"Begging the question","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"begging-the-question","label":"Begging the question","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-fine-tuner-does-not-explain","side":"con","sectionTitle":"lawful intelligibility, cosmic origins, and fine-tuning","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Dawkins concludes that postulating a fine-tuner merely relocates the fine-tuning problem unless the proposed mind’s own explanatory status and complexity receive an account.","lockedProposition":"Dawkins argues that postulating a fine-tuner merely invents away the fine-tuning problem rather than explaining it.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"we need an explanation for the fine tuning and so we postulate a fine tuner um but you haven't explained anything i mean you simply invented you've magicked away the problem and this is","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Dawkins targets the decisive comparative question—whether adding a fine-tuner produces genuine explanatory gain rather than merely naming an agent capable of selecting the observed constants. Principal limitation: Saying that nothing has been explained is too strong because personal explanation can identify intentions without reducing an agent to prior mechanics, and the move has not established that God is contingent or complex in the relevant sense. Live burden: Dawkins must justify applying the same explanatory demand to a timeless creator and engage divine-simplicity or necessary-being replies instead of assuming ordinary engineered complexity."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The claim that a fine-tuner explains nothing may assume a contested standard of explanation, but it does not directly smuggle that identical conclusion into its premises. The assessment identifies overstatement and an unmet burden instead."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-priors-mediate-explanations","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-priors-mediate-explanations","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"lawful intelligibility, cosmic origins, and fine-tuning","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that choosing between creator and multiverse explanations partly depends on prior probabilities because inaccessible universes leave the evidence underdetermining their comparison.","lockedProposition":"Because other universes may be impossible to measure, Collins argues that choosing between multiverse and creator explanations partly depends on one's prior probability for God.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"and that makes it a uncomfortable place to place all one bet so if you've got these opportunities you have a creator god on the one hand and you have a theoretical prediction of multiverses that you will never be able to measure you know pick occam's razor here which of those is a more plausible explanation kind of depends on whether you have a prior","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins concedes inflationary motivation and correctly observes that prior probabilities influence rational comparison when evidence does not decisively discriminate between hypotheses with very different ontological commitments. Principal limitation: He invokes prior belief and Occam’s razor without supplying principled priors, comparative likelihoods, or a complexity analysis, leaving the central judgment vulnerable to becoming a reflection of starting commitments. Live burden: Collins must show how fine-tuning updates reasonable priors toward God and why a creator is not more explanatorily costly than an independently generated ensemble."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Collins correctly observes that priors mediate underdetermined comparisons, but does not here selectively manipulate evidence to protect his own view. The assessment warns that unspecified priors leave the judgment vulnerable to starting commitments, which is not proof of the defined bias."}]}
{"moveId":"con-multiverse-not-ad-hoc","type":"fallacy","label":"Appeal to authority","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"appeal-to-authority","label":"Appeal to authority","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/appeal-to-authority/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-multiverse-not-ad-hoc","side":"con","sectionTitle":"lawful intelligibility, cosmic origins, and fine-tuning","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Because multiverse proposals may follow independently from reputable inflationary physics, Dawkins tentatively concludes they are not inventions devised solely to answer fine-tuning.","lockedProposition":"Dawkins tentatively understands the multiverse as a prediction of independently reputable inflationary physics rather than an invention solely for fine-tuning.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"and and i don't i'm not a physicist enough to understand that but but i would be less convinced by the multiverse if it was just invented for the purpose of solving this problem of fine-tuning but my understanding is that that's not the case that it already was a prediction or is a prediction of other reputable aspects of physics i think especially the inflation theory i may bother that wrong","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Dawkins directly answers the charge of ad hoc invention by identifying independent theoretical provenance, while repeatedly qualifying his expertise and acknowledging that his recollection could be mistaken. Principal limitation: The appeal remains secondhand and does not specify which inflationary model predicts what ensemble, whether the relevant measure problem is tractable, or how strongly present evidence supports actual universes. Live burden: Dawkins must substantiate the physical connection and show that independent motivation yields a better fine-tuning explanation rather than merely making the multiverse less improvised."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Dawkins invokes reputable physics secondhand, but does so tentatively, qualifies his expertise, and offers independent theoretical provenance rather than status alone. The assessment calls for substantiation without treating authority as settling the case."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Dawkins invokes reputable inflationary physics secondhand, but repeatedly qualifies his expertise and treats the point tentatively rather than settled by prestige. The assessment demands substantiation; it does not find authority functioning as decisive proof."}]}

{"moveId":"pro-human-capacities-fit-purpose","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-human-capacities-fit-purpose","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"suffering, natural regularity, and miracles","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that moral agency, love, beauty, and religious seeking fit a universe intended to produce persons capable of relationship with God.","lockedProposition":"The universe is also interpretable as one intended to produce morally responsible, loving, beauty-appreciating creatures capable of seeking God.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"uh i would argue that the universe has exactly the properties you would expect if it was put there by god who desired creatures like us to exist who could be morally responsible who can love and care for each other who can appreciate beauty and who will know the difference between good and evil and will ultimately seek a relationship with god the universe has those properties","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins supplies a positive purposive interpretation grounded in identifiable human capacities rather than relying solely on gaps in current scientific explanation, integrating morality, love, beauty, and religious seeking. Principal limitation: Saying the universe has “exactly” the expected properties selects hospitable evidence while omitting parasites, disasters, disease, extinction, moral failure, and rival natural explanations for the same capacities. Live burden: Collins must compare the full evidential distribution under theism and naturalism and explain why these goods are sufficiently distinctive to support divine intention."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The claim that the universe has exactly the expected purposive properties selects love, morality, beauty, and religious seeking while discounting the contrary natural harms and failures expressly identified in the assessment."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Collins says the universe has exactly the expected properties by selecting morality, love, beauty, and religious seeking while omitting parasites, disasters, disease, extinction, and moral failure. The assessment expressly identifies this one-sided evidential selection."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-lawful-creation-entails-harms","type":"bias","label":"Scope neglect","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"scope-neglect","label":"Scope neglect","definition":"Failing to adjust judgment in proportion to the scale, number, or magnitude of what is being discussed.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-lawful-creation-entails-harms","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"suffering, natural regularity, and miracles","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that stable evolutionary laws inevitably produce harmful byproducts alongside valuable outcomes, making continual divine prevention incompatible with an orderly creation.","lockedProposition":"A lawful evolutionary creation can produce both beautiful and harmful outcomes, so God cannot continually intervene to prevent every harmful byproduct.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"it to be if you're going to set natural laws in place including evolution to ultimately results in something amazing and beautiful creation it's going to also do other things along the way and god can't step in every whip","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins gives a substantive compatibility account connecting the same stable, generative laws to both beautiful outcomes and harmful side effects, rather than denying the severity of natural suffering. Principal limitation: The move blurs inability with a policy of nonintervention and does not demonstrate that these particular harms, their scale, or their distribution are necessary for lawfulness and the desired goods. Live burden: Collins must explain why an omnipotent creator could not choose better laws or limited rescues, and why regularity justifies suffering without making miracles problematic."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The reply moves from the general possibility that lawful systems have harmful byproducts to acceptance of the actual harms without adjusting for their particular scale or distribution, precisely the omission identified in the assessment."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-rare-miracles-convey-message","type":"fallacy","label":"Special pleading","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"special-pleading","label":"Special pleading","definition":"Applying an exception or different standard to one side of the argument without giving a principled reason for the difference.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-rare-miracles-convey-message","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"suffering, natural regularity, and miracles","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Because God ordinarily sustains natural laws but may exceptionally intervene to communicate, Collins concludes that rare miracles such as the resurrection remain consistent with regularity.","lockedProposition":"Stable natural law and miracles are consistent if divine intervention is an extreme, message-bearing exception such as the resurrection.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"it both ways if god was the author of natural laws and in every instance except extreme exceptions where god has a message for people that he cares about then god chooses to interfere with his own natural laws and something like the resurrection happens i don't find that","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins supplies a clear logical distinction between a stable general order and rare revelatory exceptions, thereby showing that regularity and miracles need not constitute a strict contradiction. Principal limitation: The message-bearing status and occurrence of the resurrection are asserted, and no criterion explains why communicating a message warrants intervention while rescuing suffering children or preventing disasters does not. Live burden: Collins must defend the evidence for the claimed exceptions and provide a morally coherent selection principle that preserves both divine care and ordinary regularity."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Collins proposes message-bearing intervention as a principled category of exception rather than offering no reason for disparate treatment. The criterion is unsupported and morally incomplete, but that alone does not satisfy special pleading."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Collins gives a stated principle for the exception: rare interventions communicate pivotal messages while ordinary regularity remains intact. The principle may be evidentially unsupported or morally inadequate, but it is a principled reason rather than an unexplained exemption."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-constant-intervention-destroys-order","type":"fallacy","label":"Red herring","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"red-herring","label":"Red herring","definition":"Diverting attention from the issue under dispute to a related but less relevant point, leaving the original challenge insufficiently answered.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-constant-intervention-destroys-order","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"suffering, natural regularity, and miracles","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that routine disaster-preventing miracles would destroy predictable natural order, whereas brief interventions at pivotal revelatory moments preserve its ordinary stability.","lockedProposition":"An author of natural law may suspend it at pivotal revelatory moments, whereas intervention at every hurricane would create unmanageable chaos.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"ganglions of history where something really critical needs to be conveyed to god's people yes as the author of natural laws i believe god is entirely able to decide to briefly suspend them but if he was doing that every time there was a hurricane think of the chaos around us how would we ever possibly","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins identifies the practical and epistemic value of a predictable world and explains why ubiquitous suspension of consequences could undermine the stable environment required for meaningful action. Principal limitation: Dawkins did not require intervention at every hurricane; carefully limited rescues need not produce chaos, and the response leaves unexplained why pivotal communication outranks severe suffering in the divine selection policy. Live burden: Collins must answer bounded-intervention cases and defend a principled threshold for miracles rather than contrasting revelation with an extreme scenario of constant disruption."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Dawkins's challenge concerns selective or bounded rescues, but Collins redirects it to intervention at every hurricane and the resulting chaos. The assessment expressly says this extreme leaves the bounded-intervention challenge unanswered."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Dawkins's challenge concerns selective intervention and bounded rescues, but Collins redirects it to intervention at every hurricane and the chaos of constant suspension. The assessment explicitly notes that this extreme scenario leaves the bounded-intervention challenge unanswered."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-nobility-exceeds-misfiring-description","type":"bias","label":"Belief bias","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"belief-bias","label":"Belief bias","definition":"Judging an argument by whether its conclusion feels believable instead of evaluating whether the conclusion follows from the premises.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/belief-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-nobility-exceeds-misfiring-description","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"morality and radical altruism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Because calling radical altruism an evolutionary misfiring discounts its recognized moral significance, Collins concludes that this account inadequately captures the exceptional nobility of sacrificial care.","lockedProposition":"Collins objects that describing radical altruism as a misfiring evolutionary mechanism discounts conduct humans recognize as especially noble.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"richard i have i've heard you use the analogy to sex before and i kind of see where you're going but it bothers me a bit uh that one would basically discount uh the most noble kinds of acts of human radical altruism as a misfiring of some evolutionary mechanism that's gone awry","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins protects an important phenomenological datum: radical altruism is experienced and evaluated as morally noble, not merely as behavior whose reproductive utility has become detached from ancestral conditions. Principal limitation: Nobility does not contradict a natural causal history, and the reply attributes evaluative dismissal to Dawkins before he has denied the worth or admirability of altruistic conduct. Live burden: Collins must explain why objective value or obligation is better expected under God and cannot coexist with evolutionary formation, rather than opposing moral significance to causal explanation."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Collins treats the natural explanation as inadequate because its characterization feels incompatible with altruism's acknowledged nobility, even though that value does not bear on whether the proposed causal explanation follows."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Collins treats the evolutionary account as inadequate because describing noble altruism as a misfiring feels evaluatively troubling, although nobility is compatible with a natural causal history. The assessment identifies this mistaken opposition between moral significance and explanation."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-morality-signposts-holy-god","type":"fallacy","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"argument-from-ignorance","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":"Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-morality-signposts-holy-god","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"morality and radical altruism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that recurring felt obligation to avoid evil resists purely evolutionary description and can serve as a cumulative signpost toward a holy God.","lockedProposition":"The felt obligation to avoid evil resists a purely evolutionary account for Collins and serves as a possible signpost of a holy God.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"we're supposed to avoid the evil i just have a big problem seeing that as a purely evolutionary consequence that has no deeper meaning and this is where i do get to the point of morality and richard i do see that if i was looking for a signpost of something that said that god might be interested in me and god might be the ultimate example of holiness here i find it and i'm puzzled by that","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins shifts from particular moral customs to the phenomenology of obligation and carefully frames its theistic significance as a possible signpost within a cumulative case, not a demonstration. Principal limitation: The recurrence of moral experience is asserted without detailed cross-cultural evidence, while the bridge from normativity to a holy, personally interested God remains incomplete and naturalistic rivals are insufficiently compared. Live burden: Collins must explain why evolutionary and cultural formation cannot ground or track moral reasons and why divine character improves the account without creating an independent standard problem."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Collins's stated difficulty seeing obligation as purely evolutionary becomes a signpost toward a holy, interested God without a completed positive bridge. The assessment notes that naturalistic rivals are insufficiently compared and that the theistic inference remains incomplete."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-morality-signposts-holy-god","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-morality-signposts-holy-god","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"morality and radical altruism","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that recurring felt obligation to avoid evil resists purely evolutionary description and can serve as a cumulative signpost toward a holy God.","lockedProposition":"The felt obligation to avoid evil resists a purely evolutionary account for Collins and serves as a possible signpost of a holy God.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"we're supposed to avoid the evil i just have a big problem seeing that as a purely evolutionary consequence that has no deeper meaning and this is where i do get to the point of morality and richard i do see that if i was looking for a signpost of something that said that god might be interested in me and god might be the ultimate example of holiness here i find it and i'm puzzled by that","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins shifts from particular moral customs to the phenomenology of obligation and carefully frames its theistic significance as a possible signpost within a cumulative case, not a demonstration. Principal limitation: The recurrence of moral experience is asserted without detailed cross-cultural evidence, while the bridge from normativity to a holy, personally interested God remains incomplete and naturalistic rivals are insufficiently compared. Live burden: Collins must explain why evolutionary and cultural formation cannot ground or track moral reasons and why divine character improves the account without creating an independent standard problem."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"Collins describes felt obligation and personal puzzlement as a possible signpost, but carefully stops short of treating the experience as verification. The assessment finds an incomplete bridge, not reliance on resonance as proof."}]}
{"moveId":"con-moral-terms-need-no-spirit","type":"fallacy","label":"Begging the question","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"begging-the-question","label":"Begging the question","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-moral-terms-need-no-spirit","side":"con","sectionTitle":"morality and radical altruism","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Dawkins concludes that good and evil can describe human conduct without positing God or an independently existing moral spirit beyond the natural world.","lockedProposition":"Good and evil can describe what good and bad people do without positing a divine or independently existing moral spirit.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"isn't it enough to say good is what good people do and evil is what bad people do you do you you don't need to invoke kind of spirit of god or spirit of evil it's not something hanging out there in the air it's just","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Dawkins directly challenges the claim that meaningful moral vocabulary automatically requires a supernatural entity and offers a plainly naturalistic orientation toward describing conduct. Principal limitation: Defining good as what good people do is circular, supplies no standard for identifying good people, and leaves moral disagreement, authority, obligation, and reasons for action almost entirely unexplained. Live burden: Dawkins must provide a noncircular account of normative standards and show why moral judgments bind agents without reducing Collins’s proposal to a spirit floating independently in space."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Defining good as what good people do assumes the very standard needed to identify good people, making the proposed naturalistic account circular exactly as the assessment states."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Defining good as what good people do and evil as what bad people do presupposes the very moral classifications needing explanation. The assessment explicitly calls the formulation circular and notes that it supplies no independent standard for identifying good people."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-beauty-lacks-obvious-fitness-value","type":"fallacy","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"argument-from-ignorance","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":"Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-beauty-lacks-obvious-fitness-value","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"beauty, experience, and material explanation","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that mathematical, musical, and natural beauty exceed the peahen analogy because their deep significance has no obvious direct evolutionary benefit.","lockedProposition":"Mathematical, musical, and natural beauty lacks an obvious evolutionary benefit yet matters deeply, which Collins regards as resistant to a merely peahen-like account.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"beauty uh which we touched on and which i also think can't be dismissed as we're just pee hens we see beauty and equations we see beauty in music and in a pink in this in the in the western sky at sunset and it doesn't seem to have a whole lot of evolutionary benefit to us and yet it matters and it also lifts us in a","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins broadens the aesthetic datum beyond mating preference with specific examples from equations, music, and sunsets, thereby directly exposing the limited coverage of Dawkins’s peahen analogy. Principal limitation: Absence of an obvious direct fitness advantage does not exclude cognitive byproducts, general learning capacities, social coordination, cultural evolution, or indirect benefits, and no affirmative bridge to God is supplied. Live burden: Collins must compare natural accounts of broad aesthetic cognition with a purposive hypothesis and show why deep significance carries evidential rather than solely experiential weight."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The absence of an obvious direct fitness benefit is used to resist one narrow analogy, not to establish God or the falsity of every natural account. The assessment identifies missing alternatives and warrant rather than an ignorance inference."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Collins treats the absence of an obvious evolutionary benefit as reason to resist a natural account of mathematical, musical, and natural beauty. The assessment identifies several unexcluded natural mechanisms, making the reasoning materially depend on what has not been shown."}]}
{"moveId":"con-beauty-has-natural-analogy","type":"fallacy","label":"Equivocation","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"equivocation","label":"Equivocation","definition":"Shifting the meaning of a key word or phrase during the argument, making the reasoning seem stronger than it is.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"con-beauty-has-natural-analogy","side":"con","sectionTitle":"beauty, experience, and material explanation","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because peahens exhibit aesthetic preference, Dawkins concludes by analogy that human beauty can remain wonderful and explanation-worthy while receiving a natural evolutionary explanation.","lockedProposition":"Beauty is wonderful and explanation-worthy, but analogous aesthetic preferences in peahens show that a sense of beauty can receive a natural explanation.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"that's why i raise it i mean peahens have a sense of beauty which is why peacocks look the way they do um it's it's it's it's not uh i mean it's it's it's wonderful and and it requires explanation but it has an explanation and in the same way as beauty is something which we can understand uh um the same way pee hens have a sense of beauty we can too","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Dawkins provides a concrete sexual-selection analogy showing that aesthetic preference can arise naturally and explicitly preserves wonder rather than treating explanation as a reason for dismissal. Principal limitation: Peahen mate preference covers a narrow domain and does not by itself explain human responsiveness to music, mathematics, landscapes, abstraction, cultural development, or experiences interpreted as transcendence. Live burden: Dawkins must extend the natural account across the breadth of human aesthetic experience and distinguish shared explanatory principles from a potentially loose use of “beauty” across species."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Dawkins moves from peahen mate preference to the much broader human category of beauty without preserving a demonstrated common meaning across music, mathematics, landscapes, and abstraction. The assessment expressly flags this potentially loose cross-species usage."}]}
{"moveId":"pro-joy-suggests-transcendence","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-joy-suggests-transcendence","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"beauty, experience, and material explanation","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Collins concludes that fleeting experiences of Lewisian joy suggest something beyond ordinary life and resist being treated as nothing more than neural activity.","lockedProposition":"Experiences of beauty can lift people beyond ordinary life into Lewisian joy—a fleeting sense of something more that Collins resists reducing to neurotransmission.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"matters and it also lifts us in a certain way into a plane of something other than the sort of pure dailiness of our experience some glimpse of something what lewis calls joy where you have just a sense that there's something more if i could just grasp it and then as soon as you try it's gone and that feels to me like a real significant experience that should not be dismissed as a neurotransmitter","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Collins vividly identifies a distinctive first-person phenomenon—a transient sense of “something more”—and appropriately offers it as one experiential contribution to a cumulative case rather than public proof. Principal limitation: The seeming of transcendence does not verify a transcendent object, and contrasting significance with neurotransmission wrongly suggests that neural realization would demean or exhaust the experience’s value. Live burden: Collins must show that this phenomenology discriminates theism from psychological, cultural, and neural explanations while avoiding reliance on intensity or personal meaning alone."},"blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Collins treats the personally compelling feeling that there is 'something more' as a significant suggestion of transcendence, while the assessment stresses that this seeming and private meaning do not verify a transcendent object."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"Collins treats a personally significant, fleeting feeling of something more as experiential support for transcendence. The assessment states that the seeming does not verify a transcendent object and warns against relying on intensity or personal meaning alone."}]}
